WEBVTT

00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:14,220
Is there a blood test to show that I'm getting better? How can I tell?

00:00:17,630 --> 00:00:22,230
Maybe, maybe not. So there's no perfect way to know

00:00:22,950 --> 00:00:29,510
whether our cancer is shrinking or not even in, you know, with gold standard testing that is like

00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:36,990
PET scans and MRIs or CT scans. I've always said that it's good to

00:00:36,990 --> 00:00:45,710
you know, compare apples to apples. We need that. Okay, if the size of your breast

00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:52,030
tumor was determined by an MRI, how am I going to know if it's shrinking unless I do

00:00:52,030 --> 00:00:59,270
another MRI, preferably from the same place, preferably read by the same

00:00:59,270 --> 00:01:05,910
radiologist. Now I don't know, none of those things are, you know, a given, but you're

00:01:05,910 --> 00:01:16,630
trying to be as close to comparison from size by using the same type of scans.

00:01:16,630 --> 00:01:22,910
So if you did breast ultrasound, it'd be better to go to the same ultrasound

00:01:22,910 --> 00:01:30,710
facility and get the ultrasound read that way that you're going to have close to

00:01:30,710 --> 00:01:38,710
the best comparison. Now what if you don't have a tumor that can be measured with a scan

00:01:38,710 --> 00:01:45,310
an ultrasound an MRI or CT scan? A PET scan is another way to look at things, but of

00:01:45,310 --> 00:01:49,870
course all these things that we're talking about except ultrasounds are invasive.

00:01:49,870 --> 00:01:56,070
You're getting radiation when you're doing, you know, CT scans, you're getting

00:01:56,070 --> 00:02:00,470
gadolinium usually because you have to do a contrast eye when you're doing an MRI.

00:02:00,470 --> 00:02:05,190
So that's not always our first choice. That's not even the question. The question is there

00:02:05,190 --> 00:02:11,030
a blood test? So is there a blood test to know how I'm doing? Well, if your cancer is one

00:02:11,030 --> 00:02:17,310
that shows tumor markers, that is the benefit of running tumor marker tests. So if you have

00:02:17,310 --> 00:02:24,110
a cancer that tends to show tumor markers, then if you got a baseline and your tumor marker

00:02:24,270 --> 00:02:29,990
is going down, that's a clue. So I don't think any of these tests that I'm talking about

00:02:29,990 --> 00:02:36,990
are going to be perfect. Maybe just because my tumor markers are going down does not mean

00:02:36,990 --> 00:02:43,430
that, you know, my cancer is getting better. There's other things that you look at too.

00:02:43,430 --> 00:02:49,630
But that is one indication that your tumor is getting better. If your tumor marker is going

00:02:49,630 --> 00:02:55,230
up, does that mean your cancer is getting worse? Maybe, maybe not. It's a clue that gives

00:02:55,230 --> 00:03:01,550
us, you know, reason to pause and reason to think and you compare it with other things.

00:03:01,550 --> 00:03:08,670
So as far as a blood test goes, those tumor markers are one thing that may be valuable. There's

00:03:08,670 --> 00:03:15,990
other things on the blood test that can give us clues. So if, you know, that's why I want

00:03:15,990 --> 00:03:23,350
you guys to be getting a complete comprehensive metabolic panel on a regular basis and a CBC,

00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:29,310
a complete blood count with differential on a regular basis. So if my complete blood count

00:03:29,310 --> 00:03:35,470
has been normal and you're looking at these things over time, my complete blood count has

00:03:35,470 --> 00:03:41,230
been normal over time. But now I'm starting to develop an anemia. Does that, what does that

00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:47,430
mean? That could be an indication that your cancer is progressing because as our cancer

00:03:47,430 --> 00:03:53,630
progresses, one of the things that is common, it's not always present, but it's common is for

00:03:53,630 --> 00:04:00,070
an anemia to be present because cancer can be gobbled up iron because of the high metabolic

00:04:00,070 --> 00:04:06,070
rate of the cancer. The complete metabolic, comprehensive metabolic panel, we can be looking

00:04:06,070 --> 00:04:10,030
at two liver markers. We could be looking at alkaline phosphatase. We could be looking

00:04:10,030 --> 00:04:15,350
at calcium markers. So there's things that can give us clues, but there's not going to

00:04:15,350 --> 00:04:20,390
be one thing that it's going to say, oh my gosh, your cancer is absolutely getting worse.

00:04:20,390 --> 00:04:26,310
We know we on a shadow of a doubt because of this one test. So you use blood work to give

00:04:26,310 --> 00:04:32,670
us clues to maybe dig a little deeper, plus you use subjective findings as well. Am I feeling

00:04:32,670 --> 00:04:38,870
better or am I feeling worse? So you put all these pieces of the puzzle together to come

00:04:38,870 --> 00:04:47,270
up with a diagnosis whether the progression of the cancer whether it's getting better or

00:04:47,270 --> 00:04:48,910
it's getting worse.

